commit 3ed8f8c2e57c67dad292d1d118d5bc4cde8e99f2
Author: Wasim Jalali <245747147+wasimjalali@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Sun Oct 18 20:52:15 2026 +0200

    fix: treat a written keystroke as sent, guard the paste inside its script, and settle the picker before delivery
    
    - A request that was written, or a one-shot process that was started, and then aborted,
      timed out or died is indeterminate: the dictation counts as delivered, stays on the
      clipboard and is never cancelled or reported as "Press Ctrl+V". Only a request proven
      unsent (still queued, refused, or no process ever started) can be blocked or cancelled.
      The logic moves to src/main/keystroke.ts so the process-boundary cases are tested.
    - The paste script checks, right before SendKeys, that the foreground process is not our
      own pid (unless the dictation began in our window) and that the clipboard text still
      hashes to the dictation. Only an integer and a hex hash are passed in, never the text.
    - A clipboard replaced while waiting sends nothing, leaves the newer copy alone and ends as
      a delivery error that says the dictation is saved in Useful Voice.
    - The service timeout aborts the sink and waits a bounded grace for its own verdict, so a
      keystroke that was already written is not reported as copied.
    - A dictation that began in our window pastes there only into a focused text field.
    - The language picker does not reveal after the dictation moved on, and delivery waits
      (up to 1.5 s) for a closing picker's foreground restore.
    - Timing comments are honest about host start and queue waits; the hotkey and foreground
      comments are corrected.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

