     7	 * A one-shot `powershell.exe` per call costs a process start plus an `Add-Type` C#
     8	 * compile every time (up to a second or more, cold), and the delivery path made three
     9	 * of them. Here the process starts once at app ready, the C# types are compiled once,
    10	 * and each call is a line over stdin.
    11	 *
    12	 * The protocol is one line per request: `<base64 script>|<id>`. The bootstrap below
    13	 * decodes it, runs it, and prints `UVEND:<id>` when done, so output is cut at a
    14	 * sentinel nobody else can guess. The host runs one request at a time (a queue), so a
    15	 * paste can never overlap a window read.
    16	 *
    17	 * Failure handling, because a wedged host would be worse than no host:
    18	 *  - every request has its own time limit; when the one being run exceeds it the host
    19	 *    is killed (a script that may still be about to press Ctrl+V must not outlive its
    20	 *    caller) and restarted in the background;
    21	 *  - a host that dies is restarted, but a host that keeps dying is given up on, and
    22	 *    callers fall back to a one-shot process;
    23	 *  - the error says whether the request line was written (`sent`), because only a
    24	 *    request that was never sent is safe to run again somewhere else.
    25	 *
